    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d897">

                <pb facs="1024" n="1012" xml:id="pg1012"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114A/209: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-12-15T17:00:00+01:00">December 15,
                            1943—5 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:29 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7871. American interests Japan and occupied territory, inspection of prisoner of
                    war camps. Foreign Office official handed Tait<anchor n="94" xml:id="d897-f1"/>
                    December 14 notice to the effect that Gorgé reports full accounts Japanese
                    prisoner camps never visited and certain others for example those Tokyo and
                    Yokohama have not been inspected for more than a year. This situation greatly
                    concerns Gorgé who does not miss single opportunity personally insist with
                    Japanese Foreign Office obtain necessary authorization visit. He fears prisoners
                    of war have impression he is not doing all possible being [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">bring?</hi>] moral comfort which they doubtlessly need
                    greatly.</p>
                <p>Under these circumstances he suggests British and American Governments again
                    insist authorization visit prisoners of war camps be given Swiss
                    representatives.</p>
                <p>British Legation received similar communication December 10 and telegraphed
                    London same day.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d897-f1" n="94" xml:id="d897-f1-content">
                <p>George Tait, First Secretary of Legation in Switzerland.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
